  "full_text": "So this is Excalibur. It's a space game. Oh, my ship got tweaked. I have to do a spacewalk. Oh, no, I got hit. Okay. Anyway, so God said there should be weather in space games. So this has solar wind, solar flares that you have to turn your ship away during the storm. The gun on here was inspired by the naval mines in World War II. You know how they had magnetic mines that would... Well, this is some kind of ion. I don't know. It's new physics. It's just some fictional physics. I don't know. You can imagine some kind of charged particle or something. I don't know. Oh, you've got to turn your ship away during a solar storm. And so there's the missile. So we're going to go to the laser level, which is, I think, level four. So you get on target for a few seconds. And the laser jitters because of the... the struts of the ship vibrating. Anyway, so it turns out there's a control system to bring you out of a spin. If you hit the back arrow, anyway, if you hit the back, the down cursor, you have left, right, forward, and then if you do cursor back, then it will bring you out of a spin. And it'll stabilize your laser. Anyway, just a little bit. I try to keep it difficult. So it doesn't stabilize your laser that much. So anyway, your laser overheats if you use it too long. There's a thermometer gauge at the bottom. Anyway, so the size of your ion cone or whatever the hell it is. There's a fuse and there's a size for the round. So if you set the fuse, so you can make bigger or smaller. And anyway, you can play around with it. And that's why it's called Excalibur because it's a variable diameter gun. So anyway.",
